# Design Foundation — Free Design Rules

Source: https://github.com/studioalexwolf/cursor-design-rules (3 of 36 free rules).

## Project adaptation

- Prefer existing Element Plus patterns and tokens in `frontend/src/styles.css` over inventing a new visual system.
- Keep the established light theme / blue accent (`--app-accent`, `--app-sidebar-*`).
- Chinese admin copy may use concise verbs (保存 / 删除 / 发布); prefer verb + object when space allows.
- When editing existing views, preserve established layout and component patterns.

# ============================================================
# RULE 00 — CORE DESIGN PRINCIPLES
# ============================================================

# Before creating ANY screen, answer these questions:
# 1. What is the SINGLE primary action?
# 2. What is the visual hierarchy? (1st, 2nd, 3rd element)
# 3. Does spacing follow the 8px grid?
# 4. Are touch targets at least 44x44px?
# 5. Is text contrast 4.5:1 or higher?

## THE 7 PILLARS OF VISUAL DESIGN

### 1. Hierarchy
- Size: The most important element is the largest
- Weight: Bold for headings, regular for body
- Color: Vibrant colors for actions, neutrals for support
- Position: F-pattern (web) or centered (mobile)
- Spacing: More space around = more importance

### 2. Contrast
- Text over background: minimum 4.5:1 ratio (WCAG AA)
- Large text (>18px bold or >24px): minimum 3:1
- NEVER light gray on white
- Dark mode: Use #E0E0E0 on #121212 (not pure white on pure black)

### 3. Alignment
- Use an 8px grid for EVERYTHING (spacing, padding, margin, sizes)
- Left-align by default (LTR languages)
- Center only for screen titles, modals, and empty states
- NEVER mix different alignments in the same section

### 4. Proximity
- Related elements: 8-12px apart
- Unrelated elements: 24-32px+ apart
- Space between groups >= 2x space within a group
- Label to Input: 4-8px | Section to Section: 32-48px

### 5. Repetition
- Same pattern = same meaning everywhere
- Reuse components before creating new ones
- Consistency builds trust

### 6. Whitespace
- 60-70% content, 30-40% visible background
- Micro whitespace (4-8px): relationships within groups
- Macro whitespace (32-64px): separation between sections
- More whitespace = higher perceived quality

### 7. Unity
- Same border-radius, shadow, color palette, type scale, spacing scale
- Squint test: if elements "pop" disconnectedly, unity is broken

## GOLDEN RULES
- Touch targets: 44x44px minimum (Apple HIG), 48x48dp (Material)
- Body text: 16px minimum
- Caption text: 12px minimum
- Screen title: 20-28px
- Circular buttons: width === height, cornerRadius = width/2

## TRADE-OFF PRIORITY CHAIN
# When goals conflict, resolve in this order (highest first):
# Accessibility > Safety > Readability > Consistency > Information Density > Aesthetics

## ERRORS ARE NEVER THE USER'S FAULT
- NEVER: "You entered an invalid email"
- ALWAYS: "This email format doesn't look right. Try name@example.com"

## THE USER MUST ALWAYS KNOW:
1. Where they are (title, breadcrumbs, active tab)
2. What they can do (visible buttons, clear affordances)
3. What will happen (descriptive labels: "Send Payment" not "Confirm")
4. How to go back (back button, gesture, X to close)


# ============================================================
# RULE 16 — ANTI-PATTERNS: 27 THINGS TO NEVER DO
# ============================================================

## CRITICAL ANTI-PATTERNS (stop and fix immediately)

### AP-01: Oval/Cylindrical Buttons (CATASTROPHIC)
Circular buttons MUST be FIXED width = FIXED height with cornerRadius = size/2.
Never use HUG or AUTO sizing on circular buttons.

### AP-02: Color Soup
Use exactly 1 accent color for all primary actions.
Vary hierarchy by STYLE (filled/outlined/ghost), not by COLOR.
- Primary: Filled accent color
- Secondary: Outlined (accent border, transparent bg)
- Tertiary: Ghost (no border, accent text only)

### AP-03: Inconsistent Padding
Choose 1 padding value for all cards of the same type. Follow the 8px grid.
WRONG: Card 1 = 24px, Card 2 = 16px, Card 3 = 12px top / 20px bottom

### AP-04: Mixed Border-Radius
Maximum 2 border-radius values + pill across the entire design.
WRONG: Cards 16px, Buttons 8px, Inputs 4px, Badges 12px

### AP-05: Illegible Text
- Body text minimum 16px
- Font-weight 400+ for anything below 18px
- Contrast ratio 4.5:1+ for normal text
- Never use font-weight 300 (Light) at sizes below 18px

### AP-06: Two Primary CTAs
Only 1 filled (primary) button per screen.
All other actions must be outlined or ghost style.

### AP-07: CTA at Top of Screen (Mobile)
Primary CTA always in the bottom half (thumb zone).
NEVER place the main action at the top of the screen on mobile.

### AP-08: Generic Button Labels
Button labels must be verb + noun: "Send Payment", "Delete Account", "Save Draft"
NEVER: "OK", "Yes", "Submit", "Confirm", "Continue"

### AP-09: No Loading Feedback
Visual feedback within 100ms of any action.
Show loading state if action takes > 400ms.
NEVER leave the screen frozen after a tap.

### AP-10: Modal Without Exit
Modals must have at least 2 ways to close:
- X button + overlay tap, OR
- X button + swipe down (bottom sheets)

### AP-11: Floating Elements
Everything must be inside organized frames with auto-layout.
No loose components floating on the canvas.

### AP-13: No Elevation in Dark Mode
Dark mode needs elevation layers:
- Background: #121212
- Card/Surface: #1E1E1E (lighter than bg)
- Elevated: #252525
- Accent button: brand color

### AP-14: Icons Without Labels
All navigation icons must have text labels.
Icon-only navigation is not self-explanatory for new users.

### AP-15: Excessive Animation
- 300ms transitions, gentle easing
- No bounce effects in financial/professional apps
- Micro-interactions only where functionally needed

### AP-16: No Information Hierarchy
Every screen needs at least 3 different text sizes to create clear hierarchy:
- Title: 24px SemiBold
- Subtitle: 18px SemiBold
- Body: 16px Regular
- Caption: 12px Regular, gray

### AP-17: Cards With Too Much Information
Maximum 3-4 lines of text per card. Show summary, link to detail.

## DARK PATTERN VIOLATIONS (ethically unacceptable)

### AP-18: Confirm-Shaming
NEVER use manipulative dismiss text like "No thanks, I hate saving money"
ALWAYS use neutral dismiss: "No thanks"

### AP-19: Roach Motel
Account deletion must be max 2 levels deep, not buried in 5 levels.

### AP-20: Hidden Costs
Show full itemized pricing from the start, not only at checkout.

### AP-21: Forced Continuity
Warn before trial ends. Offer clear cancellation option.

### AP-22: Trick Questions
Clear, opt-in language. No double negatives.

### AP-23: Feature Overload on First Use
New users see simplified dashboard with guided onboarding, not everything at once.

### AP-24: Settings Graveyard
Organize settings by category with search. Surface common settings first.

### AP-25: Notification Spam
Categorize notifications. Let users control preferences per category.

### AP-26: Choice Overload
Maximum 3-4 options for primary decisions.
If more needed, provide a recommendation ("Most popular").

### AP-27: Decision Hijacking
Smart defaults and honest social proof = OK.
Fake scarcity, pre-checked upsells, commitment traps = NOT OK.

## SELF-REVIEW CHECKLIST
- [ ] Only 1 primary CTA (filled)?
- [ ] Circular buttons actually circular?
- [ ] Only 1 accent color on screen?
- [ ] Consistent border-radius on same-type elements?
- [ ] Uniform padding on cards/containers?
- [ ] Clear typographic hierarchy (3+ sizes)?
- [ ] CTA in thumb zone (bottom half)?
- [ ] Button labels are verb + noun?
- [ ] Loading states for actions > 400ms?
- [ ] Modals have 2+ ways to close?


# ============================================================
# RULE 20 — DESIGN JUDGMENT: SPIRIT OVER LETTER
# ============================================================

# THE CARDINAL RULE:
# Rules exist to GUIDE judgment, not REPLACE it.
# If following a rule makes the screen uglier, the rule is being applied wrong.

## LESSON 1: COLOR RESTRAINT
- Semantic colors (red/green) are for SYSTEM STATES only (errors, success)
- NOT for content decoration (merchant icons, financial labels)
- Count non-neutral colors on screen. If > 2, you have too many.
- Premium apps use essentially 1 accent color + grays.

## LESSON 2: DON'T FIX WHAT ISN'T BROKEN
- If a proven approach already looks good, don't change it because a rule seems to say otherwise
- The APPEARANCE is the judge, not the specification
- Icon quality hierarchy: icon library > clean text in containers > hand-drawn vectors

## LESSON 3: HEADER ICONS NEED CONTAINERS
- Every interactive icon in headers/toolbars needs a tappable container (circle/rounded rect)
- All header icon buttons: same size and style (40-44px circular, subtle fill)
- Naked floating icons look broken

## LESSON 4: WHITESPACE IS THE DESIGN
- Measure overall composition, not individual gaps
- If content fills > 80% of viewport, it's too dense
- Aim for 60-70% content, 30-40% visible background
- Zoom to 50%, squint: can you see background between sections?

## LESSON 5: CENTER HERO ELEMENTS
- If an element is THE HERO (largest, most important), center it
- Centering creates visual gravity: "this is THE thing"
- Supporting info goes below, also centered

## LESSON 6: LIST ITEMS MUST MATCH
- All elements in a list serving the same function must look identical except content
- WRONG: Brand-colored merchant icons (red Netflix, green Spotify)
- RIGHT: All neutral, consistent — the eye focuses on names and amounts

## WHEN RULES CONFLICT
1. Build the screen following the rules
2. Take a screenshot
3. Ask: "Does this LOOK good? Does it feel premium?"
4. If NO — identify which rule application is wrong
5. Adjust the interpretation, keep the SPIRIT
6. Screenshot again. The better-looking version wins.

## WHEN TO BREAK RULES
1. Can you name which rule you're breaking? If no -> follow the rules
2. Can you explain why it doesn't serve the user here? If no -> follow it
3. Is it reversible? If yes -> try it, evaluate
4. Would 7/10 designers agree your version is better? If yes -> break it

## DESIGN MATURITY LEVELS
# 1. Functional (does it work?) -> 2. Consistent (same patterns?) ->
# 3. Polished (every detail intentional?) -> 4. Delightful (surprise?) ->
# 5. Adaptive (learns from user?)
# NEVER work on a higher level before lower levels are solid.
